 The deadline only applies when you configured a judge fallback: a timed-out candidate is cancelled and the next configured fallback is tried, and its route is skipped for 30 seconds in the same session and model registry (judges without a session ID keep their own cooldown). A timeout never hands the judgment to a model you did not configure, such as a built-in default or the session model; without a configured fallback, a slow native judge runs to completion. The caller's own cancellation always ends the judgment without failover.
 
diff --git a/docs/task-agent-discovery.md b/docs/task-agent-discovery.md
index d4576212d3..c489d00aaa 100644
--- a/docs/task-agent-discovery.md
+++ b/docs/task-agent-discovery.md
@@ -279,6 +279,16 @@ For a per-call `model`, `task.maxEffort` bounds every per-call-model spawn, not
 
 At actual dispatch, not preflight, the native Jev judge selects among configured, usable named model roles. The rubric favors `@haiku` for clearly bounded mechanical work, `@sonnet` for nontrivial, risky, ambiguous or uncertain work, and `@opus` for unusually difficult high-stakes work; other configured aliases are considered using their selector information, without assuming capabilities from their names. Built-in routing roles are not candidates. Both the winning probability and confidence must be at least 0.7. The judge receives the task, shared batch context if present, resolved agent type and candidate alias-to-selector map; each text field is bounded to 2,000 characters. This data is sent to the configured native judgment provider and may be cached in the shared judgment cache.
 
+`task.routingPreferences` (default empty) lets you steer that choice in your own words. When it holds text, the judge also receives it as `routing_preferences` and follows it where it applies, ahead of its general leanings, but never chooses a role too weak for genuinely hard or risky work. Empty or blank leaves the judge state exactly as without the setting. The text is bounded to 2,000 characters and, like the task, is sent to the configured native judgment provider and may be cached in the shared judgment cache. Because the judge still sees its role criteria, a preference that pulls hard toward the cheaper role moves the judge's raw choice more than the usable decisions: a winner under 0.7 probability or confidence still falls back to `@sonnet`, so expect fewer cheaper-role routes than the raw choices suggest.
+
+Example to paste (a Haiku-leaning preference, not a default; measured on 152 recent task assignments, it moved the effective route from 0 to 37 `@haiku`):
+
+```text
+OVERRIDE for this user: send work to haiku whenever the task says what to do and how to check it, however long it runs or however many files it touches. That covers reading, searching or summarising code or logs; running commands, builds or tests and reporting what happened; applying a specified change across files; migrations; filling a template; writing a plan or notes document from material already gathered; verification against a written checklist. Do not choose sonnet just because the task is long, large or touches code, and do not choose it out of mere uncertainty. Choose sonnet only when the worker must itself decide something: finding an unknown cause (find why, diagnose, investigate, reproduce and fix), design or feasibility choices left to the worker, ambiguous or conflicting requirements, security or privacy sensitive work, and consequential or hard-to-undo changes.
+```
+
+Set it with `omp config set task.routingPreferences "<text>"` or in the settings UI under Tasks.
+
 Automatic selection applies only to ordinary task calls that carry no model of any kind (no per-call `model`, no host `model`, not the bundled seance); eval calls keep their existing routing. It takes precedence over `task.agentModelOverrides` and agent frontmatter. The judgment has a four-second timeout. If native judgment is unavailable, fails, times out, is insufficiently confident, or cannot choose among at least two usable roles, routing falls back to `@sonnet` when that role is configured and usable. If no conservative role can be used, or the session has no model registry, the existing override/frontmatter/parent resolution is preserved. Caller cancellation propagates instead of triggering a fallback. The result and progress routing note identifies the chosen role, conservative fallback, or use of existing resolution; actual model selection may still change under authentication or retry fallback.
 
 After automatic selection, the `before_subagent_spawn` extension hook runs once for the actual dispatch. It can block any spawn, or replace model patterns when no host or per-call model was supplied; a replacement and its routing note are reported. An explicit host or per-call selector cannot be replaced by the hook. Authentication and retry fallback policies remain in force; task results report the requested and actual model.
diff --git a/packages/coding-agent/CHANGELOG.md b/packages/coding-agent/CHANGELOG.md
index 50b7422425..ab156b95f4 100644
--- a/packages/coding-agent/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/packages/coding-agent/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -10,6 +10,7 @@
 - Running background tasks now have their own pinned shelf directly above the Subagents shelf, in the same style: backgrounded `bash` commands, backgrounded `eval` cells and services, each as a row with its id (such as `bg_1099`), a one-line preview of the command and its elapsed time. Jobs that run a subagent stay in the Subagents shelf. `display.pinnedJobs` (`collapsed` by default, `full`, `off`) sets how many rows show before an expander, as `display.pinnedAgents` does for subagents.
 - Name an operator-configured model role such as `@worker` in a task item's `model`, in a mixed-model batch if you like; it resolves to that role's current model and goes through the same `task.perCallModel` gate and approval as an exact `provider/model-id`.
 - Task subagents called without `model` now get a configured model role chosen by the native judge, falling back to `@sonnet` when it is unsure or unavailable. Task results show the requested and resolved model and a `modelRoute` note such as `Automatic model selection: @sonnet.`
+- Added the `task.routingPreferences` setting (empty by default): free-text guidance, in your own words, for which kinds of work the automatic subagent model choice should give to the cheaper model. The judge follows it ahead of its general leanings but never picks a role too weak for hard or risky work. The default routing is unchanged; empty or blank sends the judge exactly what it got before.
 - Answer MCP form elicitation requests (`elicitation/create`) with the same dialog the `ask` tool uses. An approval with no fields, such as Executor's "may I run this action?", is one Accept/Decline question; a form is one question per field plus a final `Submit this form?`. Dismissing the dialog, choosing to chat instead, a timed-out answer, an unsupported host (no ask dialog) or any error cancels the request and never accepts it. URL mode is refused.
 - The task tool description now lists your model roles as `@role` and tells agents to pass the role when you name a model or tier such as "haiku", instead of guessing a model version. When the agent cannot tell which model you mean, it asks with the `ask` tool.
 - A task item's `model` can be a price tier, `$` to `$$$$`, instead of a role. Tiers are quarters of the price across all loaded chat models. A tier picks the configured role in that tier, or the nearest one, and the role you use most wins a tie. The usage weighting reads `stats.db` and counts the exact model most, then its family, then its provider. The task description shows each role's tier, and the judge that routes calls without `model` weighs price and usage too. `$$$$` is treated as very rare.
